🚀 2. Solution: Make ArrayList Thread-safe
-----------------------------------------------------
✅ SOLUTION 1: synchronizedList (most basic)
👉 “ArrayList is not thread-safe,
so in multi-threaded environments it can cause data inconsistency
and race conditions.
To fix this, we can wrap it using Collections.synchronizedList(),
which synchronizes all operations using internal locking.
However, iteration still needs external synchronization to avoid
concurrency issues.”

❌ PROBLEM (Non-thread-safe ArrayList):
public class unsafeExample{
List<String> transactions=new Arraylist<>();
psvm{
Runnable task=()->{
for(int i=0;i<1000;i++){
transactions.add(Thread.concurrentThread().getName()+"Transaction..."+ i);
}
};

Thread t1=new Thread t1(task);
Thread t2=new Thread t2(task);
Thread t3=new Thread t3(task);

t1.start();
t2.start();
t3.start();

try{
t1.join();
t2.join();
t3.join();
}catch(Exception e){}
sout("total transaction: "+ transactions.size()));
}
💥 WHAT GOES WRONG?
In real system:
⚠ Problems:
Missing transactions (data loss)
Inconsistent size
Sometimes exceptions
Race condition during resizing

🧠 WHY THIS HAPPENS?

Because:
ArrayList is NOT thread-safe
Internal array is modified by multiple threads simultaneously
👉 So memory gets corrupted logically

✅ SOLUTION (Thread-safe ArrayList using synchronizedList)

